WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.225
Lesson 3.1.

00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:04.040
How lending markets work.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.743
By the end, you'll explain where lending yields

00:00:06.743 --> 00:00:09.220
actually come from, and why rates jump when

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:11.135
a market is nearly fully borrowed.

00:00:12.435 --> 00:00:13.725
Here's the plan.

00:00:13.725 --> 00:00:16.334
What a money market actually is, mechanically,

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:18.910
and where the yield genuinely comes from.

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:22.625
Utilisation, and the kinked interest-rate curve it drives.

00:00:22.625 --> 00:00:24.420
A full worked example, with

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.515
a real, verified rate table.

00:00:26.515 --> 00:00:29.056
And why a sudden spike in rates near full

00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:31.840
utilisation is a warning sign, not free money.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:34.050
The mechanism.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:37.430
Here's the mechanism, in full.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:40.055
Lenders supply assets into a shared pool.

00:00:40.055 --> 00:00:42.180
Borrowers borrow from that same pool,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.550
against collateral they've locked up.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.106
A smart contract sets the

00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:48.500
rates on both sides, automatically.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:51.374
And when you supply, you receive a receipt token

00:00:51.374 --> 00:00:55.129
back, representing your deposit plus the interest it's earning,

00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:57.710
growing in value the whole time you hold it.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.757
Here's exactly where the yield genuinely comes

00:01:01.757 --> 00:01:03.803
from, since it isn't magic, and it

00:01:03.803 --> 00:01:05.915
isn't the protocol's own money.

00:01:05.915 --> 00:01:08.352
Every dollar a lender earns is a dollar a

00:01:08.352 --> 00:01:11.716
borrower actually paid, in interest, for the privilege of

00:01:11.716 --> 00:01:15.555
borrowing against their own collateral instead of selling it.

00:01:15.555 --> 00:01:17.420
No borrower, no yield.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.815
It's a direct transfer, mediated by a

00:01:19.815 --> 00:01:22.840
smart contract, not manufactured out of nowhere.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:27.225
Here's exactly what happens to your receipt token,

00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:30.005
step by step, from the moment you supply.

00:01:30.005 --> 00:01:32.154
You deposit an asset, and receive

00:01:32.154 --> 00:01:34.920
a receipt token in return, immediately.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:38.497
That token's exchange rate against the underlying asset

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:42.565
climbs continuously, as interest accrues, second by second.

00:01:42.565 --> 00:01:44.343
You never need to claim interest

00:01:44.343 --> 00:01:46.905
separately, or take any additional action.

00:01:46.905 --> 00:01:49.048
And when you eventually redeem it, you

00:01:49.048 --> 00:01:51.356
receive back more of the underlying asset

00:01:51.356 --> 00:01:52.840
than you originally put in.

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:57.752
Worth contrasting this against a traditional bank loan, since

00:01:57.752 --> 00:02:01.605
it explains why DeFi lending works so differently underneath.

00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:04.177
A bank lends based on your credit history

00:02:04.177 --> 00:02:06.932
and income, trusting you to repay over time,

00:02:06.932 --> 00:02:09.375
with no collateral locked up front.

00:02:09.375 --> 00:02:12.933
A DeFi money market instead requires collateral worth

00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:16.359
more than the loan, upfront, and enforces repayment

00:02:16.359 --> 00:02:19.060
through code, not trust or credit checks.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:21.450
Quick check.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.088
Where does a lender's yield in

00:02:23.088 --> 00:02:24.885
a money market actually come from?

00:02:29.305 --> 00:02:31.944
The answer: directly from the interest borrowers

00:02:31.944 --> 00:02:34.345
pay to borrow against their collateral.

00:02:34.345 --> 00:02:36.610
It's a transfer between the two sides,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.585
not money created by the protocol.

00:02:39.885 --> 00:02:41.440
The kinked interest-rate curve.

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:44.849
Here's exactly what utilisation is,

00:02:44.849 --> 00:02:46.965
and what it does, mechanically.

00:02:46.965 --> 00:02:50.290
Utilisation equals the amount currently borrowed,

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:53.050
divided by the total amount supplied.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:57.267
Below a market's chosen optimal point, commonly around eighty

00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:01.240
percent, rates rise only gently as utilisation climbs.

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.227
Above that same optimal point, the curve kinks,

00:03:04.227 --> 00:03:07.526
and rates rise steeply instead, specifically to pull

00:03:07.526 --> 00:03:10.264
in more lenders and push out some borrowers.

00:03:11.564 --> 00:03:15.381
Worth naming the trade-off from each side's perspective directly,

00:03:15.381 --> 00:03:19.614
since “gentle then steep” affects lenders and borrowers differently.

00:03:19.614 --> 00:03:22.246
A lender below the optimal point earns a

00:03:22.246 --> 00:03:25.136
modest, steady rate; above it, a much richer

00:03:25.136 --> 00:03:28.389
one, but with growing liquidity risk attached.

00:03:28.389 --> 00:03:31.621
A borrower below the optimal point pays a modest,

00:03:31.621 --> 00:03:34.853
predictable rate; above it, a rate that can climb

00:03:34.853 --> 00:03:38.279
sharply, fast enough to force an unplanned repayment.

00:03:39.579 --> 00:03:41.696
Worth being precise about why the curve

00:03:41.696 --> 00:03:44.077
is deliberately shaped this way, rather than

00:03:44.077 --> 00:03:45.964
a straight line the whole way.

00:03:45.964 --> 00:03:49.543
A smooth, straight-line curve gives the market no urgent

00:03:49.543 --> 00:03:53.248
signal as it approaches full utilisation, the exact moment

00:03:53.248 --> 00:03:56.374
lenders might not be able to withdraw at all.

00:03:56.374 --> 00:03:58.806
The kink is a deliberate design choice:

00:03:58.806 --> 00:04:01.543
make the pain sharp and sudden, specifically

00:04:01.543 --> 00:04:04.279
where the actual danger, illiquidity, begins.

00:04:05.579 --> 00:04:09.060
One more piece worth naming before the worked

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.617
example: supply A.P.Y. is never quite the same

00:04:12.617 --> 00:04:16.474
as borrow A.P.Y., even at the same utilisation.

00:04:16.474 --> 00:04:21.602
Supply A.P.Y. is roughly borrow A.P.Y., times utilisation, times

00:04:21.602 --> 00:04:26.084
one minus the reserve factor, the protocol's own cut.

00:04:26.084 --> 00:04:29.571
That reserve factor is exactly why lenders always earn

00:04:29.571 --> 00:04:32.994
somewhat less than what borrowers are actually paying.

00:04:34.294 --> 00:04:36.853
Here's how to actually check a market's own

00:04:36.853 --> 00:04:39.470
kink point yourself, since it isn't the same

00:04:39.470 --> 00:04:41.689
on every protocol or every asset.

00:04:41.689 --> 00:04:45.589
Open the market's own interface or documentation directly.

00:04:45.589 --> 00:04:48.873
Look specifically for its “optimal utilisation” or

00:04:48.873 --> 00:04:52.263
“kink” parameter, usually shown as a percentage.

00:04:52.263 --> 00:04:55.308
And compare that number against the market's current

00:04:55.308 --> 00:04:58.065
utilisation, so you know how much room actually

00:04:58.065 --> 00:05:00.593
remains before rates start climbing steeply.

00:05:01.893 --> 00:05:02.983
Quick check.

00:05:02.983 --> 00:05:05.823
Why does the interest-rate curve kink sharply

00:05:05.823 --> 00:05:08.786
above the optimal utilisation point, instead of

00:05:08.786 --> 00:05:10.638
rising smoothly the whole way?

00:05:15.058 --> 00:05:18.561
The answer: to make borrowing expensive and supplying

00:05:18.561 --> 00:05:22.649
attractive right when liquidity runs low, restoring withdrawal

00:05:22.649 --> 00:05:25.568
capacity before the market actually locks up.

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:27.873
Worked example.

00:05:29.173 --> 00:05:33.635
Here's the actual curve, plotted from this specific

00:05:33.635 --> 00:05:38.011
illustrative example: 4% borrow APY at 80% optimal

00:05:38.011 --> 00:05:41.658
utilisation, then a 60% slope above it.

00:05:41.658 --> 00:05:46.893
At 50% utilisation, borrow APY is 2.5%.

00:05:46.893 --> 00:05:49.783
At 70%, 3.5%.

00:05:49.783 --> 00:05:53.483
At 80%, exactly 4%, the kink itself.

00:05:53.483 --> 00:05:59.003
And then it launches: 90% utilisation reaches

00:05:59.003 --> 00:06:03.323
34% borrow APY, and 95% reaches 49%.

00:06:04.623 --> 00:06:07.471
Now put supply A.P.Y. next to that same

00:06:07.471 --> 00:06:11.317
borrow A.P.Y., at a ten-percent reserve factor, since

00:06:11.317 --> 00:06:14.038
lenders never see the full number.

00:06:14.038 --> 00:06:16.901
At eighty percent utilisation: four percent

00:06:16.901 --> 00:06:19.673
borrow, two point nine percent supply.

00:06:19.673 --> 00:06:24.460
At ninety-five percent: forty-nine percent borrow, but supply climbs

00:06:24.460 --> 00:06:28.969
just as dramatically, to roughly forty-two percent, since supply

00:06:28.969 --> 00:06:32.438
A.P.Y. scales directly with that same borrow rate.

00:06:33.738 --> 00:06:35.421
One real, dated event, so

00:06:35.421 --> 00:06:37.728
this isn't a hypothetical risk.

00:06:37.728 --> 00:06:41.625
Major lending markets have genuinely experienced episodes of

00:06:41.625 --> 00:06:45.521
utilisation pushing well above ninety percent during periods

00:06:45.521 --> 00:06:48.715
of high demand, with supply rates spiking sharply

00:06:48.715 --> 00:06:51.570
and withdrawal delays reported by users.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.305
This is outside research, not this program's

00:06:54.305 --> 00:06:56.736
own data, but it's exactly the scenario

00:06:56.736 --> 00:06:58.985
this worked example is built to help

00:06:58.985 --> 00:07:01.355
you recognise before it happens to you.

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:04.307
And here's the actual warning

00:07:04.307 --> 00:07:06.425
hidden inside that second number.

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:10.019
A sudden forty-two-percent supply A.P.Y. looks like an

00:07:10.019 --> 00:07:13.352
incredible yield, right up until you remember what

00:07:13.352 --> 00:07:16.227
it actually means: the pool is nearly fully

00:07:16.227 --> 00:07:19.167
borrowed, and lenders may simply not be able

00:07:19.167 --> 00:07:22.043
to withdraw, because the money is out, lent

00:07:22.043 --> 00:07:24.695
to borrowers who haven't repaid yet.

00:07:24.695 --> 00:07:26.605
A sudden high supply APY is

00:07:26.605 --> 00:07:28.514
often a warning, not a gift.

00:07:29.813 --> 00:07:33.588
Here's exactly what unfolds, step by step, as utilisation

00:07:33.588 --> 00:07:37.493
actually climbs toward one hundred percent, so “lenders may

00:07:37.493 --> 00:07:40.722
not be able to withdraw” isn't just a phrase.

00:07:40.722 --> 00:07:43.717
Utilisation climbs past the optimal point.

00:07:43.717 --> 00:07:46.014
Rates spike steeply, exactly as the

00:07:46.014 --> 00:07:47.782
kink is designed to do.

00:07:47.782 --> 00:07:50.695
Some lenders, seeing high rates, try to withdraw,

00:07:50.695 --> 00:07:53.792
but the pool's available liquidity is shrinking.

00:07:53.792 --> 00:07:56.553
And once utilisation is high enough, some

00:07:56.553 --> 00:07:59.839
withdrawals simply queue, or fail outright, until

00:07:59.839 --> 00:08:02.337
borrowers repay or new supply arrives.

00:08:03.637 --> 00:08:04.727
Quick check.

00:08:04.727 --> 00:08:08.532
Utilisation on a lending market is ninety-nine percent.

00:08:08.532 --> 00:08:10.807
What's the actual risk for lenders right now?

00:08:15.227 --> 00:08:19.328
The answer: withdrawals may be blocked entirely, until borrowers

00:08:19.328 --> 00:08:22.420
repay or new supply arrives, since almost all of

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:25.007
the pool's assets are currently lent out.

00:08:26.307 --> 00:08:27.767
Here's your checklist.

00:08:27.767 --> 00:08:30.542
Do it now, before supplying to any market.

00:08:30.542 --> 00:08:32.790
Check the market's current utilisation

00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.762
before you supply, not after.

00:08:34.762 --> 00:08:37.862
Know that specific market's optimal-utilisation kink,

00:08:37.862 --> 00:08:40.172
since it isn't the same everywhere.

00:08:40.172 --> 00:08:42.987
And treat any sudden rate spike as a liquidity

00:08:42.987 --> 00:08:46.402
warning to investigate, never as free money to celebrate.

00:08:47.702 --> 00:08:51.013
One last honest connection, before the recap, since this

00:08:51.013 --> 00:08:54.857
lesson sits right underneath everything else in Module Three.

00:08:54.857 --> 00:08:57.895
Every borrow you'll model in the lessons ahead,

00:08:57.895 --> 00:09:02.200
health factor, leveraged loops, perpetual futures, ultimately draws

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.795
on the exact same pool mechanism and the

00:09:04.795 --> 00:09:08.197
exact same curve this lesson just walked through.

00:09:08.197 --> 00:09:10.843
Understanding this curve well is what makes

00:09:10.843 --> 00:09:12.948
the rest of the module make sense,

00:09:12.948 --> 00:09:14.512
not a detail to skip past.

00:09:15.812 --> 00:09:17.057
Let's recap.

00:09:17.057 --> 00:09:20.393
A money market is a pool: lenders supply, borrowers

00:09:20.393 --> 00:09:24.157
borrow against collateral, a contract sets both rates.

00:09:24.157 --> 00:09:26.468
Every dollar of yield a lender earns

00:09:26.468 --> 00:09:28.841
is a dollar a borrower actually paid;

00:09:28.841 --> 00:09:31.327
it's a transfer, not created money.

00:09:31.327 --> 00:09:34.549
The interest-rate curve kinks deliberately above an

00:09:34.549 --> 00:09:38.144
optimal utilisation point, to protect withdrawal capacity

00:09:38.144 --> 00:09:40.117
right when it matters most.

00:09:40.117 --> 00:09:42.476
And a sudden spike in supply APY,

00:09:42.476 --> 00:09:45.667
near full utilisation, is a liquidity warning

00:09:45.667 --> 00:09:48.442
first, an opportunity second, if at all.

00:09:49.742 --> 00:09:53.426
Check utilisation before you supply, and treat any sudden

00:09:53.426 --> 00:09:56.902
rate spike as a warning worth investigating first.

00:09:56.902 --> 00:09:58.992
Next up, Lesson 3.2: LTV,

00:09:58.992 --> 00:10:02.207
liquidation threshold and health factor.
